<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irnerius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1050 – after 1125</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Irnerius was an Italian jurist who taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in the early 12th century and is regarded as the founder of the Bologna school of glossators. His glosses on Justinian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a> revived the systematic study of Roman law, which drew students from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and helped make Bologna the first university town of the West.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irnerius.jpg" alt="Irnerius" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luigi Serra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luigi_Serra_-_Irnerio_che_glossa_le_antiche_leggi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Irnerius, sometimes called lucerna juris, the lantern of the law, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> about 1050. At the urging of Countess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a> he turned to jurisprudence, taking the code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> as his guide. Law had been taught at Bologna before him by Pepo and a few others, and a tradition of legal study had grown up at Pavia since the mid-ninth century. After teaching briefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he returned to Bologna and founded a new school in 1084 or 1088, which came to rival the law school of Ravenna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His teaching followed the methods used for Scripture: he read aloud a section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, which the students copied, and added his commentary and explanatory glosses. At first these were brief notes between the lines, but as they grew too long he wrote them in the margins, and the marginal gloss later came into general use. The Bolognese text of Justinian's Pandects, the Littera Bononiensis, spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as students went home, with copies traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and the Vatican.</p><p>Few works can be securely given to him. An old opinion, much disputed, makes him the author of the Authentica, an epitome of Justinian's Novellae, and his Formularium tabellionum, a guide for notaries, and Quaestiones are lost. The Summa Codicis, credited to him in Herman Fitting's edition of 1894, is now widely dated to between 1130 and 1159, and the Quaestiones de juris subtilitatibus was taken from him after Hermann Kantorowicz published a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Irnerius also served the emperor. After the death of Pope Paschal II he defended the rights of Emperor Henry V in the papal election and upheld the election of the imperial antipope Gregory VIII, and after 1116 he seems to have held an imperial office. He died perhaps under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>, certainly before 1140. Largely forgotten for centuries, he was revived by German historians of the later 19th century and the celebration of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>'s eight hundredth anniversary, though Anders Winroth has questioned much of the received account.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Irnerius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its law school, traditionally dated to 1088 and associated with the jurist <b>Irnerius</b>, drew students from all over Europe and made the city famous.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-bologna">Republic of Bologna</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1098" aria-label="Bologna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Irnerius"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5687 3739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5687 3739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.49,11.34&amp;year=1098">What was here in 1098? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
